// First and foremost, tasks.json file is used to define custom tasks that can be run from the
// VS Code command palette or integrated into the build process. In this case, we are defining a
// task to compile TypeScript files using the TypeScript compiler (tsc) based on the configuration
// specified in tsconfig.json.

// If not explicitly defined, a virtual task is created while running the task in launch.json via the "preLaunchTask" property,
// which internally constructs the command like `tsc -p c:\Users\...\tsconfig.json` and cmd handles backslashes correctly.

// However, if the default terminal is set to Bash, it treats backslashes as escape characters,
// leading to a failure when running the task.

// To fix this issue, you can either change the default terminal to Command Prompt (cmd) in VS Code
// settings or explicitly define and modify the task to use "shell" type instead of "typescript"
// and specify the command to run tsc dirctly, ensuring that the path is correctly formatted for
// the terminal being used.

// The following task fails if the default terminal is set to Bash because VS code internally
// constructs a command like "tsc -p <path>" and as it is on Windows environment, it generates
// Windows-style path (c:\...) which colides with the way bash requires paths to be formatted
// (with forward slashes or escaped backslashes).

// {
//   "version": "2.0.0",
//   "tasks": [
//     {
//       "label": "tsc: build - tsconfig.json",
//       "type": "typescript",
//       "tsconfig": "tsconfig.json",
//       "problemMatcher": ["$tsc"],
//       "group": {
//         "kind": "build",
//         "isDefault": true
//       },
//     }
//   ]
// }

{
  "version": "2.0.0",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "label": "tsc: build - tsconfig.json",
      "type": "shell",
      "command": "tsc",
      // tsc automatically detects tsconfig.json in the current directory, so you can just run `tsc`
      // without any arguments. However, if you want to specify the tsconfig file explicitly, you can
      // use args as shown below. Make sure to use the correct path format for your terminal
      // (e.g., forward slashes for Bash, backslashes for cmd).
      "args": ["-p", "tsconfig.json"], // VS Code runs the task inside your workspace folder already, so you can just specify the tsconfig file name without the full path. Works in both cmd and Bash.
      // "args": ["-p", "'${workspaceFolder}/tsconfig.json'"], // Explicit quoting — very reliable — prevents Bash from touching the path.
      "problemMatcher": "$tsc",
      "group": {
        "kind": "build",
        "isDefault": true
      }
    }
  ]
}
